the Constitution.\n\nThe Supreme Court has determined that,\n\n\u2022 Clause 43(3), in its present form, is inconsistent\n\nwith Articles 12(1) and 14(1)(i) of the Constitution. However, this inconsistency would cease if Clause 43(3) is replaced and a new Clause 43(4) is inserted in the terms set out in the Determination of the Supreme Court.\n\n\u2022 Clause 169, in its present form, is inconsistent\n\nwith Article 12(1) of the Constitution, having regard also to Article 76. However, this inconsistency would cease if Clauses 169(1) and 169(3) are amended in the manner specified in the Determination of the Supreme Court.\n\n71 72\n\n2026 \u0db8 \u0dcf\u0dc0\u0dd0\u0dda\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd 12\n\n\u2022 Clauses 45 and 65, in their present form, are\n\ninconsistent with Article 12(1) of the Constitution. However, these inconsistencies would cease if Clause 45(1) is amended and the proposed provision concerning the costs of immigration facilities is inserted with appropriate numbering, as set out in the Determination of the Supreme Court.\n\n\u2022 There is no other inconsistency with the\n\nConstitution on the grounds considered. Also, the Supreme Court records the undertaking to amend Clause 174 by deleting the word \u201cwritten\u201d.\n\n\u2022 Where the amendments necessary to remove the"
