    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Speaker, during the past two years, Sri Lanka has been able to achieve significant progress in global rankings, including economic and social rankings.\n\n\u2022 According to the 2026 Fitch Ratings, Sri Lanka has\n\n\u2022 According to the Corruption Perceptions Index\n\n(2025/2026) issued by Transparency International, Sri Lanka has advanced 14 places in the global rankings to 107th place.\n\n\u2022 According to the Global Peace Index (2026), Sri\n\nLanka advanced 14 places to be ranked 67th globally.\n\n\u2022 According to GlobeTrotter & Flash Pack, in 2025,\n\nSri Lanka was ranked first in the world index under the solo travellers category.\n\n\u2022 The World Economic Forum has named Sri Lanka\n\n10th in the world among the countries that have shown the fastest progress among tourism economies.\n\n\u2022 The international magazine \u201cCond\u00e9 Nast\u201d has named\n\nSri Lanka the best destination in the world for the whole family to travel together.\n\n351 352\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d85\u0db1\u0dd4\u0dbb \u0d9a\u0dd4\u0db8\u0ddc\u0dbb \u0daf\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0ddc \u0ddc\u0dba\u0d9a \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0ddc ]\n\n2026 \u0db1 \u0ddc\u0dc0\u0dd0\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 12\n\n\u2022 According to the assessment carried out by the\n\nInstitute of International Finance - IIF -, among 57 emerging market and developing economies, Sri Lanka secured fourth place for progress in improving investor relations and fifth place for progress in improving transparency on public debt.\n\n\u2022 The World Bank had classified Sri Lanka as a lower\n\nmiddle-income country in 2020, down from the status of an upper middle-income country. Reflecting the recovery of the economy, the classification was upgraded again in 2026 to an upper middle-income country.\n\nHon. Speaker, the above facts are not the only criteria that measure the success of our fiscal policy. The Government's support for and care of distressed children, the elderly, persons with disabilities, those suffering from chronic illnesses, low-income households and families who have lost their livelihoods due to disasters are equally important."
