    "md5": "f1668dfd64043022ee313de3eb5ab3f7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1819,
    "chunk_text": "The Central Government debt stood at 114 per cent in 2022, the year in which the economy was plunged into crisis. What is taken into consideration in debt sustainability is the capacity to repay debt relative to GDP and Government revenue, and whether there is adequate liquidity to settle the debt servicing obligations falling due in the near term.\n\n353 354\n\n\u0db4\u0ddc\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nmaintain debt sustainability. In 2025, the Central Government debt ratio had reached 95 per cent. Total foreign debt service amounted to US Dollars 3,707 million in 2025 and US Dollars 3,591 million in 2026. Going forward, it will be US Dollars 2,986 million and US Dollars 3,574 million in 2027 and 2028, respectively. We have the stability required for this debt servicing.\n\n2.2 Accelerating Economic Growth Driven by\n\nInvestment, Production and Exports\n\nOur target is to accelerate the structural transformation towards a high-productivity, export-competitive production economy. Expanding domestic and foreign private investment, diversifying export products and markets, integrating with international value chains and promoting industries and services with higher value addition are the main areas of this.\n\nWe are taking steps to broaden access to finance for small and medium-scale enterprises and to establish a clear and transparent investment environment for investors.\n\n2.3 Enhancing Productivity through Technology,\n\nInnovation and Human Capital\n\nWe give priority to enhancing national productivity by strengthening education, competency development, research, innovation and digital transformation, modernizing public services, expanding digital infrastructure, encouraging the use of new technologies and strengthening collaboration among educational institutions, research institutions and industries."
  },
