    "chunk_text": "A transformative change in the energy sector is required for the future economic transformation, national security and the improvement of the living standards of the people. The plans required for a new transformation in the energy sector, aimed at ensuring a stable and uninterrupted energy supply and reducing production costs, have been included in this Budget.\n\n6.1 Electricity\n\nToday, around 62 per cent of our installed capacity comes from renewable energy. Plans have been prepared to increase this to 69 per cent by 2029.\n\n\u2022 The installation of two synchronous condensers of 75\n\nMVA (megavolt-amperes) each at the new Habarana and Mannar locations, which will help maintain grid stability when renewable energy is added to the national system at a high percentage, and the installation of STATCOM facilities with a capacity of 300 MVAr at the Padukka 220 kV grid substation are expected to commence in 2027 and be completed in 2029 with the financial assistance of the World Bank.\n\n\u2022 A Wide Area Monitoring System will be established\n\nunder the World Bank Technical Assistance Project to identify unstable conditions in the system in advance and to make prompt and effective decisions in emergencies.\n\n\u2022 We have already introduced Battery Energy Storage\n\nSystems \u2013 BESS - to store the surplus electricity we receive during the daytime. In 2026, 160 MW is received by the system through BESS. Plans have been made for this quantity to be 560 MW in 2027 and for the total quantity to be 1,245 MW by 2029.\n\n\u2022 The price selected in the first phase (160 MW) of\n\ncalling bids for the BESS system in 2026 was Rs. 2.033 million per megawatt per month. In the second phase in 2026, when bids were called for 250 megawatts, the bid received had decreased to Rs. 1.808 million per megawatt per month. Accordingly, through competitive bidding, it has been possible to reduce the price by around 11 per cent within this year itself.\n\n\u202