    "chunk_size_bytes": 2027,
    "chunk_text": "\u2022 Rs. 500 million has been allocated for the rehabilitation of 77 existing coastal early warning towers, the construction of 120 new land-based towers covering flood, landslide and tsunami-prone areas in 25 districts, and the establishment of a multi-hazard early warning communication system.\n\n\u2022 A Dam Safety Act will be introduced to provide the legal safeguards necessary to ensure the safety of major dam systems.\n\n22. Health Services\n\nCapital expenditure of Rs. 131 billion has been allocated for maintaining and improving the public health service so that every citizen is able to obtain the quality health services they require through all medical systems administered by the Central Government and the provincial councils.\n\n\u2022 A large number of vacancies existed due to the non-recruitment of health staff over a long period. In order to deliver a quality health service, the required human resources have been recruited, namely 1,797 doctors, 303 indigenous medical practitioners, 64 dental surgeons, 3,441 nurses, 952 public health midwives and 3,415 support staff in various areas.\n\n\u2022 The pilot project of 39 \"Arogya Wellness Centres\", aimed at providing patient-centred health services close to between 5,000 - 10,000 of the population, is being successfully implemented, and it is expected to construct 1,000 centres during the period 2027 \u2013 2029 with an investment of Rs. 17,440 million jointly with the provincial councils. Rs. 3,600 million has been allocated for the year 2027 for this purpose.\n\n\u2022 In response to the requests of the people of those areas and to provide an optimal service at the level of national hospitals, it is proposed to\n\n387 388\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d85\u0db1\u0dd4\u0dbb \u0d9a\u0dd4\u0db8\u0ddc\u0dbb \u0daf\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0ddc \u0ddc\u0dba\u0d9a \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0ddc ]\n\n2026 \u0db1 \u0ddc\u0dc0\u0dd0\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 12\n\ntransfer to the Central Government the District General Hospitals of Mannar, Mullaitivu, Vavuniya, Kilinochchi, Gampaha and Avissawella, which are administered by the provincial councils, out of the 20 District General Hospitals."
  },
