    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "268fa0065d259c75c81ac51f0e85a83a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1931,
    "chunk_text": "Additionally, we propose to review certain stringent procedural requirements which could lead to taxpayers denied legitimate tax refunds or input tax credits due technical or procedural noncompliance while ensuring that adequate safeguards remain in place to protect government revenue.\n\n36.10 Revision of the Special Commodity Levy\n\n(SCL) on Several Essential Food Items\n\nHon. Speaker, with the aim of minimizing the cost-of-living pressure on consumers caused by rising prices of essential food items, it is proposed to reduce the Special Commodity Levy on imported sprats from Rs. 100 to Rs. 10 per kilogram, on Maldive fish from Rs. 302 to Rs. 10 per kilogram, and on powdered and unpowdered dried chillies by Rs. 50 per kilogram.\n\nThese tax revisions are scheduled to be implemented with effect from November 13, 2026.\n\n36.11 Phased Reduction of Import Tariffs\n\nHon. Speaker, it is proposed to reduce by 25 per cent, the existing CESS rates applicable to 800 selected Harmonized System (HS) code lines, under the Government's programme of phased reduction of import tariffs with the aim of simplifying the Import Tax structure, reducing trade-related costs and enhancing the competitiveness of the economy.\n\nIt is proposed to remove the 10 per cent Ports and Airports Development Levy \u2013PAL- currently charged on the importation of newsprint classified under specified HS codes, with effect from January 1, 2027.\n\n36.12 Supporting the Domestic Sugar Industry\n\nto Compete in the Market\n\nTo protect the domestic sugar industry, it is also proposed to increase the Special Commodity Levy imposed on imported sugar by Rs. 10 per kilogram, in order to minimize the tax disparity between domestically produced sugar and imported sugar, create equal competitive opportunities and a fair competitive environment for both parties, and ensure a more neutral and equitable tax treatment.\n\n36.13 Abolition of the Tourism Development Levy"
  },
