    "chunk_index": 492,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "a7079f16a98319f08ad3808a513a8b23",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1684,
    "chunk_text": "To protect the domestic sugar industry, it is also proposed to increase the Special Commodity Levy imposed on imported sugar by Rs. 10 per kilogram, in order to minimize the tax disparity between domestically produced sugar and imported sugar, create equal competitive opportunities and a fair competitive environment for both parties, and ensure a more neutral and equitable tax treatment.\n\n36.13 Abolition of the Tourism Development Levy\n\n413 414\n\n\u0db4\u0ddc\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nDevelopment Levy currently charged on the turnover of tourism establishments.\n\nIn order to ensure continued funding for tourism promotion and development, steps will be taken in the future to formulate a suitable financing mechanism. Until then, the required funds will be provided by the Treasury on requests made by the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority.\n\n36.14 Amendments to the Excise Ordinance\n\nThe 2025 Budget proposed to introduce a new Excise Act in place of the century-old Excise Ordinance with the aim of improving revenue management.\n\nHowever, until a new Act is introduced, we propose to bring amendments to the Excise Ordinance by further simplifying this complex process and incorporating amendments into the existing Ordinance itself.\n\n37. Borrowing Limit\n\nThe borrowing limit of the Appropriation Bill for the financial year 2027 is presented in Annexure IV. Summary of New Expenditure Proposals Presented in the 2027 Budget is given in Annexure II. Further, the documents to be presented with the Second Reading of the Budget under the Public Financial Management Act, No. 44 of 2024 have also been tabled.\n\n38. Supplementary Estimate for Ditwah Reconstruction \u2013 2027"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-12-2026-11-12",
