    "chunk_text": "Finally, Madam, let me speak about the moral heart of this Budget, how it treats the working people and the most vulnerable citizens of this country. Critics claimed that strict fiscal discipline w10ould mean abandoning the poor and freezing public wages. This Budget proves them wrong. We have allocated Rs. 130 billion towards revising public sector salaries and pensions, recognizing that an efficient, incorruptible Public Service must be fairly compensated.\n\nThen, let us look at how we protect those who need the State most. Over Rs. 230 billion has been allocated under Aswesuma to shield the low-income households. I do understand that some people claim that this is a waste of money as these families are forever dependent on that money. It is true and I understand that we should not let them be dependent on it; we must allow them to stand on their own feet. We must understand that a Budget is not just about numbers; it is not a balancing game of numbers, but it must serve the people. When we say develop the economy, that means we must create a happier nation. Therefore, it is important to have a safety net for those who are unfortunate and who might fall through the cracks of social inequalities. For that purpose, we will continue this programme.\n\nWe have allocated money for the housing initiative \u201cA Place of Your Own - A Beautiful Life\u201d and also made major investments in our Primary Healthcare Centres. For example, for Suwa Seriya alone, Rs. 1.7 billion has been allocated. We have also allocated nearly Rs. 3 billion to replace the existing ambulances because we look at the safety and health aspects of the population.\n\nMadam, a Budget is, ultimately, a statement of a government's values. The Budget, 2027 values honesty over corruption, production over speculation and inclusion over privilege. It asks every sector of society to contribute according to its capacity, while guaranteeing that the fruits of recovery are shared by all."
