    "chunk_size_bytes": 2098,
    "chunk_text": "Under the Ayurveda Act, No. 31 of 1961 (with the Amendment made by the Ayurveda (Amendment) Act, No. 19 of 2023), has provided legal recognition to the term \u201cAyurveda\u201d by incorporating traditional indigenous medicine - Deshiya Chikitsa - within its scope.\n\n3. Public Administrative Structure.\n\n629 630\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 (\u0dc0\u0ddb\u0daf\u0dca\u200d\u0dba\u0dba) \u0db1\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0db1\u0dca\u0daf \u0da2\u0dba\u0dad\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0dca\u0dc3 \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf ]\u0dc3 \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf ]\n\n2026 \u0daf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dc0\u0dd0 \u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 13\n\nspecifically to serve the indigenous medical sector, and action is being taken through these institutions to maintain indigenous medical services and ensure the preservation of their quality.\n\n4. Regulation through National Policy.\n\nFormulation of a specific National Traditional Medicine Policy, covering a period of ten years, with the objective of preserving traditional medical systems in Sri Lanka.\n\n5. Registration and Preservation of Physicians.\n\nThe Ayurveda Medical Council registers traditional practitioners who have come through traditional/Gurukula systems of training, in accordance with Section 55(1) of the Ayurveda Act, No. 31 of 1961. In accordance with the Regulations formulated by the Ayurveda Medical Council under the provisions of the Act, a mechanism is maintained to conduct written and oral examinations to determine whether the relevant applicants have acquired knowledge, experience and skills required to practise Ayurveda medicine efficiently to the satisfaction of the Council and to register such applicants who pass the examinations.\n\n6. Preservation of Traditional Knowledge,\n\nFormulations.\n\nImplementation of special projects for the preservation of traditional knowledge, formulations and palm-leaf manuscripts.\n\n7. Conservation of Herbal Plants and\n\nMaterials.\n\nMaintenance of herbal gardens and conducting indigenous medical clinics at community level for the conservation of herbal plants and materials.\n\n8. Provision of Medical Services.\n\nProvision of indigenous medical services to the public through government Ayurveda hospitals, research hospitals, teaching hospitals and outpatient treatment centres."
